data.content:
## What happened

C# 14 introduces new **span conversion rules** that change overload resolution. When you write `array.Contains(num)` on an `int[]`, the compiler now binds to **`MemoryExtensions.Contains(ReadOnlySpan<T>, T)`** instead of `Enumerable.Contains<T>(IEnumerable<T>, T)`.

`MemoryExtensions.Contains` is a span-based method that **cannot be represented in a LINQ expression tree**, so it throws at runtime (an `InvalidOperationException` or `NotSupportedException`).

## How to fix

Force the compiler to pick `Enumerable.Contains` by making the receiver explicitly `IEnumerable<T>`. Any of these work:

```csharp
// Option 1: Cast to IEnumerable<int>
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);

// Option 2: Use .AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 3: Call Enumerable.Contains explicitly
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

All three prevent the span overload from being selected, keeping the expression tree valid.
